ogygia is small on purpose. Once you hold five words in your head, every feature is a combination of them. This page is the map; the rest of the docs are the territory.

## The page ships no framework

A normal SvelteKit page boots the Kit client: it hydrates the whole tree, so every page carries the framework whether it needs it or not. ogygia flips that. You set `csr = false`, and the page is **just HTML**. No Kit client, no app-wide hydration.

What ships instead is a **~7.6 KB runtime** — a custom element (`<ogygia-region>`) plus an optional router. It sits and waits. JS arrives only for the components you explicitly mark.

```ts
// src/routes/+layout.ts — the whole page opts out of the Kit client
export const csr = false;
```

## Five words

Everything you build is one of these:

| Word | What it is | How you write it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Page** | Server HTML, no client framework. | `csr = false` |
| **Island** | A component that becomes interactive — gets JS on a schedule. | `import C from './C.svelte' with { wake: 'load' }` |
| **Lake** | Static HTML *inside* an island. Zero JS for that part. | `with { wake: 'none' }` used inside an island |
| **Server island** | HTML fetched from the server after load. Placeholder first, no JS. | `with { fill: 'load' }` |
| **Partial** | A server-*chosen* renderable you place like data — the server picks the component. | `region(Card, props)` + `<Region of={x} />` |

`hydrate` and `defer` are the two import attributes. Everything else in that table is plain English.

## When things happen

Islands and server islands share the same four timing words, so you only learn them once:

| Value | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `load` | Right away |
| `idle` | When the browser goes idle |
| `visible` | When scrolled into view |
| `'(max-width: 600px)'` | When the media query matches |

On an **island** the word says *when JS runs*. On a **server island** it says *when the HTML arrives*. Same words, two axes. See [Timing & nesting](/docs/islands/timing-nesting).

## One nesting rule

Walk up the tree. The closest marked parent decides what a component is.

```
page → island → lake → island → …
```

- Island on the page → gets its own JS.
- Island inside an island → shares the parent's JS (one interactive tree, not two).
- Lake inside an island → stays static HTML; its JS never ships.
- Island inside a lake → gets its own JS again.

That single rule is the whole nesting model. The details live in [Timing & nesting](/docs/islands/timing-nesting).

